ants, three women and a young man, were arrested as her alleged accomplices. Under torture, they confessed to dozens of killings. Three were executed within days. But B\u00e1thory was never tried. The king pressed for a trial. Thurz\u00f3 prevented it. Her powerful family wanted no public trial. And many witnesses could only repeat what they had heard. She died in confinement. Then someone added the detail that would make her infamous. More than a century after her death, a priest wrote that she bathed in murdered girls' blood to keep her beauty. No witness against her had described that. No court ever heard her. But the legend had its monster: the Blood Countess. <break time=\"0.8s\"/> History remembers the victors. We remember the rest.", "src_md5": "aedad170c314f0f03f4e4ff402c567aa", "voice": "jfIS2w2yJi0grJZPyEsk", "model": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.5, "similarity_boost": 0.75, "style": 0.0, "use_speaker_boost": true}, "seed": 1611}